	Obtain an event for a DTLS[rx]
apifn	sm_dtlsrx_get_event
params	sm_dtlsrx_event_parms eventp
	The DTLS[rx] 
field	tSMDTLSrxId dtlsrx in
	The event identifier
field	tSMEventId event out
unstruct
	If the call completes successfully 
ref	event
	will hold a pointer to the tSMEventId belonging to 
ref	dtlsrx.
	The tSMEventId is valid until the DTLS[rx] is destroyed using 
fnref	sm_dtlsrx_destroy.
	This event will be signalled when a status change occurs on the DTLS[rx].
	When the event is signalled the user must call 
fnref	sm_dtlsrx_status
	to discover the nature of the status change.
explain
	0
retval
error	ERR_SM_DEVERR - device error

	Determine DTLS[rx] status
apifn	sm_dtlsrx_status
params	sm_dtlsrx_status_parms statusp
	The DTLS[rx] to interrogate
field	tSMDTLSrxId dtlsrx in
enumstart kSMDTLSrxStatus
	Indicates that there is nothing significant to report
enum	kSMDTLSrxStatusRunning
	Indicates that the DTLS[rx] has stopped processing DTLS and that
	it can be safely destroyed
enum	kSMDTLSrxStatusStopped
	Indicates that a DTLS packet is available to be read.
enum	kSMDTLSrxStatusHasPacket
	Data has been lost because the packet buffer has overflowed.
enum	kSMDTLSrxStatusOverrun
enumend status out
unstruct
	Returns the current status of the DTLS[rx] or an error to indicate that
	an error has occurred.
	<p>
	When the event, obtained from
fnref	sm_dtlsrx_get_event,
	is signalled the user must call this function to determine the nature of
	the status change. The change in status may indicate that an error occurred
	whilst processing a user request or it may be notifiying the user of a
	change to the previous state of the DTLS[rx].
explain
	0
retval
error	ERR_SM_DEVERR - device error
error	ERR_SM_NO_RESOURCES - if insufficient resources existed to create the DTLS[rx]

	Instructs a DTLS[rx] to stop processing DTLS
apifn	sm_dtlsrx_stop
params	sm_dtlsrx_stop_parms stopp
	A tSMDTLSrxId that has been previously created by a call to
fnref	sm_dtlsrx_create.
field	tSMDTLSrxId dtlsrx in
unstruct
	Requests that a DTLS[rx] stops executing. The user will be notified that
	a DTLS[rx] has terminated by a final status event. Once the final status
	notification has been received the DTLS[rx] can be destroyed using
fnref	sm_dtlsrx_destroy.
	<p>
	Once the DTLS[rx] has stopped the event should no longer be used to wait
	for status notifications as it will be signalled permanently. The event
	is invalidated when
fnref	sm_dtlsrx_destroy
	is called.
explain
	0
retval
error	ERR_SM_DEVERR - device error

	Configure the basic options for DTLS[tx] to use IPv4
apifn	sm_dtlstx_config
params	sm_dtlstx_config_parms configp
	The DTLS[tx] to configure
field	tSMDTLStxId dtlstx in
	The SOCKADDR_IN structure specifying the destination IP address
	and port for the DTLS packets. A struct SOCKADDR_IN must be
	configured with an address family, an IP address and a port.
	Note that most operating systems define this structure such that
	fields are in network byte order.
	The structure must be correctly cast such that an IP V4 
	address is specified.
field	SOCKADDR_IN destination in
	The SOCKADDR_IN structure allows you to specify the source IP
	address and port for the DTLS data. A struct SOCKADDR_IN must
	be configured with an address family. The IP address may be
	specified, or the value INADDR_ANY may be used to indicate
	that any suitable address may be used. The port number may be
	specified, or the value zero may be used to indicate that a
	suitable port number is to be automatically allocated and
	used.
	Note that most operating systems define this structure such that
	fields are in network byte order.
	The structure must be correctly cast such that an IP V4 
	address is specified.
field	SOCKADDR_IN source in
unstruct
	Configures a DTLS[tx] to send DTLS packets to a device with the 
	specified IPv4 address.
explain
	0
retval
error	ERR_SM_DEVERR - device error

